    "conclusions": [
      "Both methods select two characteristic profiles and two migration groups among 3,050 counties. Agreement between methods is moderate (adjusted Rand index 0.548 for characteristics and 0.448 for migration), so the group boundaries depend on the chosen geometry.",
      "The higher-income, education and housing-cost characteristic profile has higher median inflow and outflow rates (51.31 and 48.05) than the other profile (41.97 and 40.88). Median net migration differs by only 0.70 per 1,000, and the middle halves of the net-migration distributions overlap substantially. The characteristic profiles primarily distinguish two-way turnover.",
      "Clustering inflow and outflow directly separates higher-turnover and lower-turnover counties. Their median net rates are 7.81 and 0.62 per 1,000, respectively. These outcome contrasts follow the rate-based grouping; the characteristic analysis compares migration after fitting. All reported rates describe IRS-covered individuals per 1,000 ACS residents."
    ]
  },
  "pca": {
    "year": 2023,
    "counties": 3050,
    "conclusions": [
      "PC1\u2013PC5 retain 80.7% of variation in the 13 standardized county characteristics. PC1 and PC2 alone retain 54.5%.",
      "PC1, the income, education and housing-cost dimension, accompanies greater two-way movement: inflow rho = 0.309 and outflow rho = 0.335, with a weaker net association (rho = 0.083).",
      "PC3, combining rainfall, age and incident history, has the strongest net-migration association among the retained components (rho = 0.362). PC4 and PC5 show weaker negative net associations; PC2 has weak associations with all three outcomes. These are descriptive patterns within the observed county cohort."
    ]
  },
